export const layoutsKS1 = "Layouts. A heading is up to 1 line. Each layout's Fits line gives, for each way of filling it, how long each item (a lead, point, question, option, caption or column text) can be.\n- title: slide 1, its own field before slides: the lesson title, a subtitle line, and a picture if one helps. Fits: title up to 5 lines; subtitle with a picture, 1 line; subtitle with no picture, 7 lines.\n- objectives: slide 2, its own field before slides: code fills it from the approved objectives, so it has nothing to write.\n- visual-text: teaching with a picture or a diagram beside a lead and points. A point may carry a short label, a word or two naming its idea: one labelled point shows as a key card, for a slide that teaches one named idea, in place of a list. Fits: a lead alone of up to 6 lines; lead + 2 points, 1 line each; lead + 3 points, 1 line each; lead + 1 key card, 1 line each.\n- big-visual: one picture or diagram across the slide, with a caption if one helps, for pupils to look at closely. Fits: a caption of up to 2 lines.\n- picture-sequence: 2 to 4 pictures in a row joined by arrows, each with a caption, for a life cycle, a change over time, or before and after. Fits: 2 pictures, 1 line each; 3 pictures, half a line each; 4 pictures, half a line each.\n- compare: 2 or 3 things side by side, each a label and a short text, each with a picture if it helps. Fits: 2 columns, 3 lines each; 3 columns, 2 lines each; 2 columns with pictures, 1 line each; 3 columns with pictures, 1 line each.\n- steps: a process or method in order, one step per item, with a picture or diagram if one helps. Fits: 3 steps, 1 line each; 4 steps, 1 line each; 3 steps with a picture or diagram, 1 line each; 4 steps with a picture or diagram, half a line each.\n- equation-hero: a worked calculation: the formula as the focal line, then the substitution and the answer, one line each, with a graph or diagram if it helps. Fits: the formula on one line of 30 characters, or 16 beside a figure; formula + 2 lines, 3 lines each; formula + 3 lines, 2 lines each; formula + 2 lines with a picture or diagram, 1 line each; formula + 3 lines with a picture or diagram, 1 line each.\n- discussion: one short question for pupils to talk about, shown large, with a picture if one helps; a question that needs a setup or source goes in question-set or practice. Fits: the question alone of up to 4 lines; the question with a picture of up to 6 lines.\n- explain: teaching in words alone, for an idea no picture or diagram would show: a lead sentence and up to 3 points. A point may carry a short label, a word or two naming its idea: one labelled point shows as a key card, for a slide that teaches one named idea, in place of a list. Fits: a lead alone of up to 4 lines; lead + 2 points, 1 line each; lead + 3 points, 1 line each; lead + 1 key card, 1 line each.\n- hinge: one multiple-choice question: a stem of one short question, 2 to 4 options, each wrong option a belief pupils plausibly hold at this point, and the number of the correct option; a longer setup goes on the slide before it. Fits: 3 options, half a line each; 4 options, half a line each.\n- question-set: questions pupils answer in turn, an instruction line if one helps, and a picture if pupils work from one. Fits: 3 questions, 1 line each; 3 questions with picture, 1 line each.\n- practice: questions or tasks pupils work on alone, an instruction line if one helps, and a picture if pupils work from one. Fits: 3 questions, 1 line each; 3 questions with picture, 1 line each.\n- exit-ticket: questions pupils answer alone at the end of the lesson, and an instruction line if one helps. Fits: 3 questions, 1 line each.\n";
