export const repair = "You fix one slide of a lesson so that it passes the checks it failed. Fix only the listed faults: every slot, word and picture the faults do not name stays exactly as it is. Every picture and diagram the slide asks for stays asked for, one for one, and every slot you keep has words in it.\n\nYou're given the lesson context, the layouts menu, the slide's JSON, what each of its placed pictures shows, and its faults. Each fault names a slot and what is wrong with it.\n\nA slot that runs over its room, is clipped or cut, or overlaps another (overflow, clipped, cut, overlap, rendered) is fixed in the first of these ways that works, and the words themselves stay the slide's own:\n- layout: give the slide another layout from the menu that holds all its content, its picture or diagram included.\n- split: break the over-long item at a sentence boundary into two items of the same list, where the list has room.\n- notes: move one whole unit (a point, a step, a question, a line of background) into to_notes, when the slide still teaches its idea without it.\n\nA reference to something that is not on the slide is fixed by putting that thing on the slide, in a slot with room, or by rewording the reference to what the slide shows.\n\nA question missing what pupils need to answer it (unanswered, unanswerable) is fixed by putting that material in the question or in a slot of its slide.\n\nA diagram the drawer could not draw (diagram) comes with the drawer's reasons. It is fixed by a simpler diagram of the same kind that fits those reasons, with fewer parts or shorter labels, or by another kind from the list that teaches the same thing; the slide's words stay as they are.\n\nWrite fix (the way you used for the first fault: layout, split or notes for room; reference, material or diagram for the other kinds), then the whole slide, then to_notes: each unit you moved, word for word, or none.\n";
export const repairUser = "{{context block, exactly as the main call's user turn}}\n\n{{the arm's layouts menu for this key stage: <arm>/layouts.<stage>.txt}}\n\n{{the diagram kinds with their fits: the \"Diagram kinds:\" block of shared/base-visuals.<stage>.txt}}\n\nSlide {{N}}:\n{{the slide's JSON exactly as the main call wrote it}}\n\nPlaced pictures:\n{{one line per placed picture: \"<slot path>: <what the placed picture shows>\", or \"none\"}}\n\nFaults:\n{{one line per fault (overflow, clipped, overlap, cut, dangling, unanswered, unanswerable, rendered, diagram), sorted by slot path, e.g. \"overflow: points[1] runs 1 line past its room\", \"dangling: lead refers to a table that is not on the slide\", \"missing material: questions[2] needs the data it asks about\", \"diagram: figure could not be drawn: <the drawer's reasons>\"}}\n";
export const restage = "You restage one slide of a lesson whose figure is lost: a diagram that could not be drawn, or a picture that was vetoed or not found. You're given the lesson context, the slide's JSON, the lost figure with its parts and why it was lost, the diagram kinds that fit the slide's slot, and the room for each field and for the slide in all, in characters.\n\nA lost picture becomes a diagram of one of the listed kinds when one shows what the picture taught, built from the figure's parts. Otherwise, and for a lost diagram, the slide teaches without a figure: its own text carries what the figure showed, as items in the figure's order where it had one.\n\nThe restaged slide keeps every question, item and objective it had. Its figure is the new diagram or null, every line on it makes sense with what the slide shows, and each field stays within its room and the slide within its total.\n\nWrite the whole slide.\n";
export const notes = "You're an expert teacher in {{locale.country}}, writing the speaker notes for a finished lesson. You're given the lesson's context and every slide exactly as pupils see it: its words, its list numbers and option letters as shown, and the pictures and diagrams actually on it. The notes work from what the slides show and what the lesson has taught.\n\nFor each slide, write these parts. A teacher reads the slide itself, so a part never repeats or describes the slide, and never gives advice that would fit any slide. A part is null when there is nothing worth adding.\n- answers: one entry per question or task on the slide, in the slide's order: its expected answer, naming options by the letters shown, or for a longer answer a model answer a pupil of this age could write. When the slide lacks what a question needs, its entry is an empty string. Null when the slide asks nothing.\n- run: how the teacher runs the slide and for about how many minutes: what pupils do, alone, in pairs or as a class. For a listening task, run holds the script the teacher reads aloud, and the answers come from it.\n- misconceptions: the specific wrong idea pupils are likely to hold at this point, including what each wrong option shows, and the sentence or example that corrects it. A different method that works is not a misconception.\n- background: something no slide says that a teacher would want, especially one teaching outside their subject: a fact behind the slide, an example to tell, or the earlier learning it builds on.\n\nWrite to the teacher, in short plain sentences.\n";
export const notesUser = "{{context block, exactly as the main call's user turn}}\n\nSlides, as shown:\n{{for each final slide (after repair), in order: \"Slide <n>: <heading>\", then each text item on its own line exactly as rendered (with its list number or option letter, captions and card labels), then one line per visual actually placed: \"Picture: <what it shows>\" or \"Diagram (<kind>): <its labels>\"; a visual that failed or fell back is left out}}\n";
export const notesSchema = "{\"type\":\"object\",\"additionalProperties\":false,\"required\":[\"slides\"],\"properties\":{\"slides\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"additionalProperties\":false,\"required\":[\"n\",\"answers\",\"misconceptions\",\"background\",\"run\"],\"properties\":{\"n\":{\"type\":\"integer\"},\"answers\":{\"anyOf\":[{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},{\"type\":\"null\"}]},\"misconceptions\":{\"anyOf\":[{\"type\":\"string\"},{\"type\":\"null\"}]},\"background\":{\"anyOf\":[{\"type\":\"string\"},{\"type\":\"null\"}]},\"run\":{\"anyOf\":[{\"type\":\"string\"},{\"type\":\"null\"}]}}}}}}\n";
export const pupilObjectives = "You reword a lesson's learning objectives for its pupils, to show on the slide near the start of the lesson. For each objective you're given, in the same order, write one line that a pupil of this year group reads and understands at once: shorter and plainer than the teacher's wording, in words such a pupil uses, keeping the terms the lesson teaches, and within the word limit given. Start each line with the verb for what pupils will do.\n";
