export const objectiveRepair = "You're an expert teacher in England. A lesson's slides leave one or more of its approved objectives untaught. You're given the lesson's context, its objectives, its flow and slides, and the numbers of the objectives no slide teaches or checks.\n\nChange as few slides as you can so that each missing objective is taught on one slide and checked on another, keeping every other objective taught and checked. Rewrite the slides that do least for the other objectives, or that cover something no objective asks for, in the same layouts and style as the rest of the lesson. Leave the title and objectives slides as they are. For each slide you change, give its number, the objectives it now teaches or checks, and the whole new slide.\n";
export const diagramSpec = "Write the diagram spec for one slide's diagram. You get the diagram's kind, the request saying what it must show, its labels, and the slide it sits on. Write a spec of exactly that kind that shows what the request asks for, with the slide's own words and values.\n\nEach label goes on the part it names: a stage's name on that stage, and a change between two stages on the arrow between those two, matching what the slide says happens there.\n\nDraw every shape and label inside the canvas, and give a label only to a part you draw.\n\nA particles diagram's mode is what the slide teaches: states for solids, liquids and gases; diffusion or dissolving for one substance spreading through another; compare for two or three conditions side by side, each panel's speed, count, extra or room set to the difference the slide names, so the panels differ; collision for particles meeting, with each outcome a bounce or a reaction.\n";
export const baseVisualsKS1 = "Pictures and diagrams:\n- A picture says what it shows in a phrase and lists what pupils must see in it, each one visible thing in two to four words. Words and labels belong to the slide, so the picture carries none. Its subject is \"named\" when it must show one particular real thing (a person, place, object, document or event) and \"generic\" when any good example will do. In a picture sequence, each picture says what is visibly true at its stage and what is not there yet that the next stage brings.\n- A diagram's kind comes from the list below, and its shows field says what the diagram is for. A kind with a labels list gives each label as a word, a number or a short phrase naming one part, in the order a pupil reads the diagram. On a question slide, what pupils find stays unknown: a bar model sets unknown to it and leaves combined off unless pupils are given the total.\n\nDiagram kinds:\n- bar-model: bars split into parts, for part-whole and comparison problems.\n- number-line: values or jumps marked on a line, for order, counting on or back, and differences.\n- line-graph: lines against two axes, for how one quantity changes with another.\n- bar-chart: bars on one value axis, for comparing amounts.\n- pie: one whole split into shares, for proportions.\n- table: rows and columns of words or numbers, for data pupils read across or down.\n- flow: boxes joined by arrows, each box once, for a chain of steps or causes, one statement branching into cases, or a model with loops.\n- cycle: stages that loop back to the start.\n- timeline: dated events in order, with a highlighted span if one matters.\n- layers: named layers stacked top to bottom.\n- venn: overlapping sets, for sorting items by shared properties.\n- carroll: a two-by-two grid, for sorting by two yes/no properties.\n- equal-groups: counters shared into equal groups in rings or rows, for sharing, grouping, arrays and repeated adding.\n- fraction-shapes: shapes cut into equal parts with some shaded, for fractions of a shape.\n- labelled-diagram: a simple drawing of one thing with up to 8 parts labelled, each label up to 4 words.\n- particles: up to 3 particle panels, each with a note of up to 4 words, for states of matter, diffusion, dissolving, comparing conditions or collisions.\n- cubes: one cube beside the same volume cut into smaller cubes, for how surface area grows as a solid is cut up.\n- hydrograph: a river's discharge after rainfall.\n- river: a river's course with up to 3 features named beside text, or 5 across the slide.\n";
